            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\r\n\r\nI think having these as options is too much. 'mini.ai' already has enough default mappings.\r\n\r\n> An alternative could be exposing directly the `expr_mapping` function, and allow users to make their own mappings while keeping the base options simpler.\r\n\r\nIts main functionality is exported via [`MiniAi.move_cursor()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/59ecf78491f29a9ec31f4124f3da77091f37e4d9/doc/mini-ai.txt#L582). It can be used almost as is with help of `vim.fn.getcharstr()` to get textobject identifier from the user. However, covering more quality-of-life features also doesn't look extremely complex to have in one's config:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nlocal make_ai_move_rhs = function(side, search_method)\r\n  return function()\r\n    local ok, tobj_id = pcall(vim.fn.getcharstr)\r\n    if not ok or tobj_id == '\\27' then return end\r\n    local tobj_esc = vim.fn.escape(tobj_id, \"'\\\\\")\r\n    local opts = string.format('{ n_times = %d, search_method = \"%s\" }', vim.v.count1, search_method)\r\n    local cmd = string.format('MiniAi.move_cursor(\"%s\", \"a\", \"%s\", %s)', side, tobj_esc, opts)\r\n    return '<Cmd>lua ' .. cmd .. '<CR>'\r\n  end\r\nend\r\n\r\nlocal map_ai_move = function(lhs, side, search_method)\r\n  vim.keymap.set({ 'n', 'x', 'o' }, lhs, make_ai_move_rhs(side, search_method), { expr = true })\r\nend\r\n\r\nmap_ai_move('g(', 'left', 'cover_or_prev')\r\nmap_ai_move('g)', 'right', 'cover_or_prev')\r\n```\r\n\r\nClosing as not planned.",
